They recounted the second part of their journey to visit the Taj Mahal. On the overnight train from Delhi to Agra, they lay on their reserved upper berth while most passengers sat below, feeling absurdly like an emperor overseeing the car. A man served them free vegetarian curry, the first free thing they had gotten in India, though they worried it would make them sick. Because the conductor made no announcements and they couldn't see out the low windows, they kept recruiting fellow passengers, an eighteen-year-old, two soldiers, and a young woman, to help track the stops, and eventually the four of them chanted "Agra! Agra!" to wake them. They caught a taxi to their guesthouse and arranged a 6 a.m. pickup. The next morning the driver dishonestly dropped them near the West Gate instead of the East Gate, but they walked quickly to the entrance. When the doors opened, they caught their first glimpse of the white marble dome through the Great Gate and spent nearly two hours photographing and touring the Taj at dawn, admiring the calligraphy, minarets, and lattice screens as mist blanketed the Yamuna River behind it. As they finished, they noticed a young blond French woman named Sophie being asked to pose in photos by Indian tourists who seemed more interested in her than the monument. They struck up a conversation, traded travel stories, and liked each other immediately, chatting by the reflection pool for about half an hour. But realizing they were headed in opposite directions, they shared an awkward moment knowing this was the only meeting of their lives. Sophie said goodbye, walked past them in front of the rising sun, glanced back one last time with a smile, and then turned her gaze toward the world's most beautiful symbol of eternal love.